WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Chapter one established a rapid route from a gut sensor cell to the vagus.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.360
How can that route teach sugar preference when sweet taste is absent?

3
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.693
First separate two measurements.

4
00:00:16.073 --> 00:00:19.393
Initial attraction is what the mouse chooses at the first sip.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.933
Learned preference is how that choice shifts after signals arrive from the gut.

6
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:30.483
Researchers matched sugar and a calorie-free sweetener for early attraction.

7
00:00:32.043 --> 00:00:34.263
Naive mice initially drank at similar rates.

8
00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:38.003
Then the sugar bar rose over the choice period.

9
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:42.527
Suppose sweet taste alone creates that later preference.

10
00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:47.707
What should happen in mice that cannot signal sweetness during the choice period?

11
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.073
It still develops.

12
00:00:56.473 --> 00:01:01.033
Mice lacking the sweet receptor or a taste-transduction channel still preferred

13
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:06.723
sugar later. In mice, learned sugar preference can develop without sweet-taste

14
00:01:06.723 --> 00:01:07.723
signalling.

15
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:14.187
In Tan and colleagues' assay, gut-delivered sugar activates vagal neurons that

16
00:01:14.187 --> 00:01:15.707
acesulfame K does not.

17
00:01:17.027 --> 00:01:21.927
Blocking SGLT1 sharply reduces glucose responses.

18
00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:28.767
Intestinal SGLT1 function is needed for this gut sugar signal.

19
00:01:29.817 --> 00:01:35.337
Silencing sugar-activated brainstem neurons prevents the learned preference but

20
00:01:35.337 --> 00:01:36.757
leaves initial attraction.

21
00:01:38.177 --> 00:01:42.997
Activating another population can create preference for a previously less-preferred

22
00:01:42.997 --> 00:01:43.997
stimulus.

23
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:49.750
A later experiment targeted CCK-labelled duodenal sensor cells.

24
00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.950
Silencing them strongly reduced the preference advantage above chance.

25
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.370
Initial attraction and learned preference are different measurements.

26
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:02.943
Sugar is not the only nutrient lesson.

27
00:02:04.503 --> 00:02:06.723
Fat preference survives loss of fat taste.

28
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:13.323
Imaging then reveals broad nutrient responders and a distinct fat-only vagal

29
00:02:13.323 --> 00:02:20.223
population. The broad population also answers sugar and amino acids, so fat is not

30
00:02:20.223 --> 00:02:22.123
carried only by fat-only cells.

31
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:30.467
Remove SGLT1, and gut sugar responses disappear while fat responses remain.

32
00:02:31.807 --> 00:02:35.567
Remove combinations of fat receptors, and fat responses fall.

33
00:02:36.827 --> 00:02:37.987
The inputs can separate.

34
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:41.147
Here is the guiding idea in action.

35
00:02:42.547 --> 00:02:47.807
Match each claim to its link: epithelial sensor, vagal response, brainstem learning,

36
00:02:48.027 --> 00:02:51.307
or behaviour. Do not merge their timescales.

37
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.870
These are mouse circuits.

38
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.030
Germline deletions do not identify the required cell type.

39
00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.190
Silencing labelled populations is acute but not perfectly pure.

40
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.750
Human behaviour remains untested here.

41
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.880
The moving picture leaves exact genotypes, sample sizes and the preference

42
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.240
calculation to the text.

43
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.020
It also leaves a harder naming problem: three different GLP-1 routes.
